The integrand is $\le\min(y^2/2,2\xi^{-2})$. For $y\ne0$ substitute $s=|y|\xi$ to get $|y|\int_0^\infty(1-\cos s)s^{-2}ds$;
  integrating by parts ($\frac{d}{ds}(-s^{-1})$, boundary terms vanish) gives $\int_0^\infty\frac{\sin s}{s}ds=\frac\pi2$
  (Mathlib: <code>integral\_sin\_div</code> / Dirichlet integral, improper). Finally $-\frac y2+\frac{|y|}2=\max\{-y,0\}$.

Apply the smooth-transition bounds to the explicit quadratic defining $\eta$.
  Use the properties of $\rho$ for $\chi$. Both terms in $\varphi$ agree near zero;
  away from zero, $|\xi|^{-p}$ is smooth. Zero extension across that neighborhood is smooth.
